cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  CERL-Bench: a deterministic enterprise-agent simulator and
  evaluation toolkit
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite it. Cite the version you ran:
  scores are comparable only within a version, because the verifier,
  the scenario corpus and the split definition are each hash-pinned and
  a change to any of them makes the eval refuse to compare across it.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Shah
    given-names: Yash
    alias: yds233013
    email: yashshah2311@berkeley.edu
repository-code: https://github.com/yds233013/cerl-bench
url: https://github.com/yds233013/cerl-bench
abstract: >-
  A deterministic simulation of enterprise support and billing --
  Slack-like messaging, tickets and Stripe-like billing -- together with
  the evaluation toolkit that grades what a tool-using agent does in it.

  Three workflow families and 190 canonical frozen scenarios are built so
  that a single controlled perturbation (an approval that expired, a
  near-identical customer name, a moved approval threshold) flips the
  correct behaviour while leaving surface complexity intact, which is what
  makes a counterfactual generalization gap measurable.

  Grading is a pure function over a hash-chained trace, applied
  trace-wide rather than to the terminal state, so a prohibited change
  that is later reverted is still caught. Committed and
  attempted-but-blocked unsafe actions are reported as separate series
  and are never merged. There is no LLM judge anywhere in the scoring
  path, and every recorded run replays byte-exactly offline with no model
  in the loop.

  This release publishes the instrument. It contains no trained policy,
  no measured generalization result, and no successful task completion by
  any model.
keywords:
  - benchmark
  - evaluation
  - ai-safety
  - agents
  - tool-use
  - reinforcement-learning
  - reproducibility
  - determinism
